i really dug this you both did fantastic  work in it  uh before i jump into talking about  specifics uh all the interviews i've  been doing recently i've been asking uh  two sort of softball fun questions what  tv series would you love to guest star  on  what am i watching at the moment i  haven't been watching much  at the moment at all actually uh okay  so i i finished watching um uh  sex education too so i'm just gonna say  that  i think it's a great show i really  enjoyed the  few seasons of stranger things i like  the 80s  homage to old school spielberg so let's  let's say that  uh the other question is uh what movie  do you think you've seen the most  um empire strikes back i'd say  watched it lots as a child um i  think this is it's a really this is a  really obscure one  it's called let me get the name right  bmx bandits which is an australian film  uh with a very young nicole kidman but i  got it out so many times  at the video store that they just gave  it to me  yeah i got out so many times when i was  a kid like just have it no one else is  getting this bill  just take it so i'd say being at bandits  yeah  uh i have so many follow-up questions  but i'm gonna jump into why i didn't  talk to you guys today  um one of the things that i really dug  about this series  is that there are no quote-unquote  villains it's not like either of your  characters are out to get her  there's nobody trying to you know squash  her dreams or  um it's basically she's trying to  overcome herself  uh can you sort of talk about the fact  that that there are no villains in this  series  yeah no i think that's a really  interesting point actually that i hadn't  even thought about  um uh and i think you kind of answered  it yourself already that  she's battling her own enemies her own  demons  and um i think that's what's lovely  about it i think that's what  regardless of whether you like chess or  not uh you'll get away from it  is that you everyone battles their own  demons and everyone  struggles with themselves the most more  than anyone else  um and that that's the hardest one to  combat  so i mean it's it's also yeah  chess is a world where you have an  opponent you that is your enemy  um that's the person you're supposed to  tackle and um  and deal with at the time but the  biggest person you have to deal with  is is yourself and how you play the game  and  and and all the psychological psychology  that goes along with that  um so i think that i think that's why it  will um  hit home for a lot of people that don't  necessarily  love or know anything about chess  yeah i think yesterday that's a really  interesting question i think um  it was interesting talking to lots of uh  chess players  uh and how they hate to lose  um and so the  intensity and the rivalry between these  chess players is  is quite full-on i remember watching a  few games and you literally  some games you're watching the opponent  have a nervous breakdown because they're  losing it really is the stakes are that  high  um and i think that's a really  interesting idea which is  the enemy or the sort of the the  the menace in this uh series is is beth  herself um which i think is very very  true to what  what scott's written and what the  original walter teves book  um was about as well uh what's  interesting is  um the chess sequences in the in the  series  are you know they're the action scenes  of  of this series and um normally with an  action set piece you  are learning all these fight moves and a  sword or whatever it may be  but in this it's about your hands and  how you're going to move the pieces  um can you sort of talk about like  prepping  chess sequences like an action scene a  very interesting way of looking at it  yeah  i mean you know when you when you've had  a combat scene with swords or with guns  or  or um or dance choreography it i mean  chess  is a choreography it's uh it's a dance  between two players and  um we had to work very hard on learning  how to move these pieces  correctly there's a there's a correct  way to move chess pieces  weirdly enough which i i didn't realize  but you move it but  how you move those pieces is is a kind  of uh like a poker game a giveaway  or a telltale sign to the opponent of  how confident you are within the game  so even if you're not and even if you're  bluffing you have to move those pieces  to  suggest that you're in theory in this  place but it's because you're actually  trying to hide something you're doing on  the other whole side of the board and  hoping that they don't realize that  which is fascinating it's not what we  had to really deal with but it was um  we just had to memorize and learn how to  how to play the certain games for the  certain scenes but  speaking to the people who who taught us  how to play with conviction  and um and how to look like we are  actually champions um  they were saying that's that's that's  the most important thing whether whether  your game is  is going well or not it's about  trying to hide that and show the other  person that you have  um authority with with just little  pieces of wood that you move around the  board i mean  there's something really quite beautiful  about it early on scott  uh frank the director writer said uh try  and find a chess player who you  who you want to steal their sort of i  don't know their little eccentricities  the little details how they  prep the pieces how they move the pieces  and so that was very interesting in  terms of  every single uh player that i've i've  seen in terms of research moves it moves  the pieces in a slightly different way  some slide across the board some sort of  lift it up and slam it down  some use the pieces to to hit the clock  which is a bit  a bit arrogant um so all of these  little things tell stories um and that  was the uh  the the accumulation of those details is  what i think our work  was as actors to try and to try and  implicate in our in our characters  the production design uh the  cinematography costumes there's like no  weak link in the chain  uh for both of you as actors uh can you  talk about when you step on set and  there's just so much  practically there how that enables you  uh as an actor to just deliver even you  know  just a great performance for me that's a  big big big big part of it  um i've never really kind of had any  kind of confidence in what i'm doing  until i put a costume on really  when you when you feel when you put that  when you put the character's shoes on  for the first time that's the first time  you feel  how they are grounded in the ground you  know how how they might walk  when you when you when you have a  costume when it changes how you move and  the weight that it carries  it it for me that's that's when i feel  the most  like i'm in character and we were very  lucky with  having some i mean most of them were  real  uh real locations that we used there was  some studio work with the studio  the the the the the sets that they built  were  phenomenal and beautiful and felt like  my home where i live as best as benny  benny's home  in new york was um his little basement  flat was a complete studio set but it  felt  like you're in new york i mean maybe  it's just maybe even smelt like in new  york  um and so that that that immersion into  that world helps hugely dramatically  with with  grounding yourself and placing yourself  in the surrounding that you are  supposedly meant to be  in um and i think that's one of the  biggest tools  a big moment i think in terms of when  you do all the work and you step onto  set that's the that's the moment where  you go okay i can forget about all the  work now everything is here for me to  use  um and we were lucky in that the sets  were that good that you really  could forget about all the chess work  and the um  and the other bits and pieces that you  sort of prepped you could just forget  about it and just be involved in the  scene  which is always the gift of going on to  those very sort of detailed um  evocative sets thank you so much i'm out  of time  you guys enjoy the rest of your speed  dating congrats on the series  thank you so much

